\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained analysis of the elementary threshold calculation on a triangular face and place it within the general probabilistic machinery used for random discrete structures. For three independent Bernoulli sites of density $p$, the probability that at least two are open is $C(p)=p^3+3p^2(1-p)=3p^2-2p^3$. We prove complement duality $C(1-p)=1-C(p)$, bounds $0\le C(p)\le1$, and the sharp trichotomy relative to $1/2$. Thus $p=1/2$ is the unique local self-dual parameter. The probability that three vertices of a triangular face are connected by independent open bonds is the same polynomial, although this local identity does not equate the infinite site and bond models. We also develop a finite independent-edge probability space and prove normalization, fixed-pattern independence, the union bound, expected-count identities, and first- and second-moment criteria. We distinguish these finite results from the planar and limiting arguments required for infinite-volume criticality. In particular, no accepted closed analytic form is known for the square-lattice site threshold.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Percolation studies connectivity in a random medium. In site percolation, vertices are independently open with probability $p$; in bond percolation, edges are independently open. An open path uses only the corresponding open objects. For an infinite lattice, a standard critical parameter is
\[
p_c=\inf\{p\in[0,1]:\Prob_p(\text{an infinite open cluster exists})>0\}.
\]
Exact values are exceptional. A local symmetry may nominate a candidate, but planar topology, sharp probability estimates, and an infinite-volume limit are needed to prove that candidate is the global threshold.

This paper isolates the exact finite calculation on one triangular face. It is the local algebra associated with the exact triangular-site critical value $1/2$, but it is not alone a proof of that infinite-lattice result. This distinction is essential for the square lattice: square bond percolation has exact threshold $1/2$, while square site percolation has a numerically known value near $0.592746$ and no accepted closed exact expression.

We then present finite moment methods. The first moment proves nonappearance when expected counts vanish; the second proves appearance when means diverge and variances remain controlled. These methods supply a general probabilistic engine for threshold arguments.

\section{Bernoulli configurations and the triangular event}
Let three independent Bernoulli sites occupy the vertices of a triangle. Call the face locally crossing when at least two sites are open.

\begin{definition}[Triangular site-crossing polynomial]
For $p\in\mathbb R$, set
\[
C(p)=p^3+3p^2(1-p).
\]
For $p\in[0,1]$, this is the probability that at least two of three independent sites are open.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Cubic form]
For every real $p$,
\[
C(p)=3p^2-2p^3.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The all-open state has mass $p^3$. There are three states with exactly two open sites, each of mass $p^2(1-p)$. Expanding their sum gives
\[
p^3+3p^2(1-p)=3p^2-2p^3.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Probability bounds]
If $0\le p\le1$, then $0\le C(p)\le1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The defining summands are nonnegative. The upper bound follows from complement duality below, since $C(p)=1-C(1-p)$ and $C(1-p)\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Complement duality and the fair point}
\begin{theorem}[Exact complement duality]
For every real $p$,
\[
C(1-p)=1-C(p).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Complementing all three states changes the Bernoulli parameter from $p$ to $1-p$. Because three is odd, ``at least two open'' becomes exactly its complementary event. The same identity follows by substituting $1-p$ into the cubic and expanding.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fairness at one half]
\[
C\left(\frac12\right)=\frac12.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Complementation fixes $p=1/2$, so $C(1/2)=1-C(1/2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sign factorization]
For every real $p$,
\[
C(p)-\frac12=(2p-1)\left(p(1-p)+\frac12\right).
\]
If $0\le p\le1$, the second factor is strictly positive.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expansion gives $3p^2-2p^3-1/2$. On the unit interval, $p(1-p)\ge0$, so the second factor is at least $1/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local threshold trichotomy]
For $0\le p\le1$,
\[
C(p)<\frac12\iff p<\frac12,\qquad
C(p)=\frac12\iff p=\frac12,
\]
and
\[
C(p)>\frac12\iff p>\frac12.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the sign factorization, $C(p)-1/2$ has the same sign as $2p-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Local triangular criticality]
A parameter $p$ is locally critical for the triangular site event if $0\le p\le1$ and $C(p)=1/2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Unique local self-dual parameter]
A real parameter is locally critical for the triangular site event if and only if $p=1/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the equality case of the local threshold trichotomy.
\end{proof}

The word ``local'' is indispensable: fairness on one face is not a definition or proof of infinite-lattice criticality. Notice also that $C'(p)=6p(1-p)$, so $C$ is strictly increasing on $(0,1)$.

\section{Bond spanning on a triangular face}
Place independent bonds on the triangle's edges. All three vertices are connected exactly when at least two bonds are open.

\begin{definition}[Triangular bond-spanning polynomial]
Set
\[
B(p)=3p^2(1-p)+p^3.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[One-face site--bond identity]
For every real $p$,
\[
B(p)=C(p)=3p^2-2p^3.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
There are three successful bond states with exactly two edges open and one with all three open. Their total mass is $3p^2(1-p)+p^3$, identical to the site count.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unique fair bond parameter]
For $0\le p\le1$,
\[
B(p)=\frac12\iff p=\frac12.
\]
\end{corollary}

The identity is only local. Site configurations randomize vertices, while bond configurations randomize edges. Gluing triangular faces creates different overlap structures, so the infinite models are not thereby identified.

\section{A finite independent-edge probability space}
Let $A$ be a finite set of $N$ potential edges. A configuration is $S\subseteq A$. Under independent inclusion with parameter $p$, define
\[
\mu_p(S)=p^{|S|}(1-p)^{N-|S|}.
\]
For an event $\mathcal E\subseteq 2^A$, put
\[
\Prob_p(\mathcal E)=\sum_{S\in\mathcal E}\mu_p(S).
\]

\begin{proposition}[Nonnegativity and normalization]
If $0\le p\le1$, then $\mu_p(S)\ge0$ and
\[
\sum_{S\subseteq A}\mu_p(S)=1.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Group subsets by cardinality and use the binomial theorem:
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{N}\binom Nk p^k(1-p)^{N-k}=(p+1-p)^N=1.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-pattern independence]
For every fixed $T\subseteq A$,
\[
\Prob_p(T\subseteq S)=p^{|T|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The edges of $T$ contribute $p^{|T|}$. Summing freely over the complementary edges contributes
\[
\sum_{R\subseteq A\setminus T}p^{|R|}(1-p)^{N-|T|-|R|}=1.
\]
\end{proof}

\section{Union bounds and the first moment}
\begin{theorem}[Finite union bound]
For finitely many events $\mathcal E_i$,
\[
\Prob_p\left(\bigcup_i\mathcal E_i\right)\le\sum_i\Prob_p(\mathcal E_i).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each configuration in the union contributes its nonnegative mass at least once on the right. Intersections cause only repeated counting.
\end{proof}

Let $\mathcal T$ be a finite family of target edge sets and define
\[
X_{\mathcal T}(S)=|\{T\in\mathcal T:T\subseteq S\}|.
\]
For a random variable $X$, define
\[
\E_p[X]=\sum_{S\subseteq A}\mu_p(S)X(S).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Expected pattern count]
\[
\E_p[X_{\mathcal T}]=\sum_{T\in\mathcal T}p^{|T|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write the count as a sum of indicators, interchange the two finite sums, and use fixed-pattern independence for each indicator.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First-moment appearance bound]
\[
\Prob_p(X_{\mathcal T}>0)\le \sum_{T\in\mathcal T}p^{|T|}=\E_p[X_{\mathcal T}].
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The positive-count event is the union over $T\in\mathcal T$ of $\{T\subseteq S\}$. Apply the union bound and the fixed-pattern theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[First-moment vanishing criterion]
For a sequence of finite systems, if $\E[X_n]\to0$, then $\Prob(X_n>0)\to0$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Variance and the second moment}
Define
\[
\Var_p(X)=\E_p[(X-\E_p[X])^2].
\]

\begin{theorem}[Zero-event second-moment inequality]
If $0\le p\le1$ and $\E_p[X]\ne0$, then
\[
\Prob_p(X=0)\le\frac{\Var_p(X)}{\E_p[X]^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On $\{X=0\}$, the squared deviation equals $\E_p[X]^2$. Retaining only those nonnegative summands in the variance gives
\[
\Var_p(X)\ge\E_p[X]^2\Prob_p(X=0).
\]
Division by the positive squared mean completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Analytic squeeze]
Suppose $0\le q_n\le V_n/E_n^2$, $E_n\to+\infty$, and $V_n\le K E_n$ for a fixed $K$. Then $q_n\to0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
One has $0\le q_n\le K/E_n$, whose upper bound tends to zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Second-moment appearance criterion]
Let $X_n$ be random variables on finite independent-edge spaces. If
\[
E_n=\E[X_n]\to+\infty,
\qquad
V_n=\Var(X_n)\le K E_n
\]
for a constant $K$, with every $E_n\ne0$, then
\[
\Prob(X_n=0)\to0.
\]
Thus $X_n\ne0$ with probability tending to one.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the zero-event inequality for each $n$ and then the analytic squeeze lemma.
\end{proof}

The first and second moments describe opposite sides of a threshold. A vanishing expected witness count proves absence; a diverging count with controlled fluctuations proves presence.

\section{Algorithms and numerical experiments}
The local polynomial can be evaluated in constant time and space. Enumerating all $2^3$ site states supplies an independent audit: sum $p^k(1-p)^{3-k}$ over states with $k\ge2$. A numerical implementation should check
\[
C(p)=B(p),\qquad C(1-p)=1-C(p),
\]
and the sign trichotomy throughout a grid in $[0,1]$.

For $N$ potential edges, exact pattern enumeration visits $2^N$ configurations. With $m$ target patterns and direct $O(N)$ subset checks, its worst-case running time is $O(2^N mN)$. Streaming configurations keeps auxiliary storage at $O(mN)$. This is suitable for small validation problems. Structured calculations should instead use the expected-count formula and group variance contributions by pairwise overlap types.

For larger finite regions, Monte Carlo simulation generates Bernoulli states and tests crossings by breadth-first search or union--find. With $M$ independent trials, the standard error of a Bernoulli estimate is at most $1/(2\sqrt M)$. Simulation estimates a transition but cannot prove an exact closed form.

\section{From local symmetry to infinite volume}
A proof of an infinite triangular-site threshold requires several bridges beyond the local theorem:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item Define finite triangular and hexagonal patches, boundary arcs, and primal and dual crossing paths.
\item Prove a deterministic planar alternative between an open primal crossing and an appropriate closed dual crossing.
\item Establish monotonicity and correlation inequalities for Bernoulli product measures.
\item Obtain crossing estimates uniform across scales and aspect ratios.
\item Use pivotal sites and Russo-type differentiation to establish sharpness.
\item Pass through increasing exhaustion and compactness from finite crossings to infinite clusters.
\end{enumerate}
Only this full chain converts local self-duality into global criticality.

The model distinctions are consequently sharp. Triangular-lattice site percolation has exact critical probability $1/2$. Square-lattice bond percolation also has exact critical probability $1/2$, by a separate planar bond-duality argument. Square-lattice site percolation has a numerical threshold near $0.592746$, with no accepted closed analytic expression. The triangular cubic cannot be transplanted to the square-site model.

\section{Conformal invariance}
At two-dimensional criticality, macroscopic crossing laws can become insensitive to microscopic scale and transform naturally under conformal maps. For critical triangular-site percolation, the scaling interfaces are associated with $\mathrm{SLE}_6$.

This conclusion lies far beyond one-face duality. One must construct discrete exploration paths, prove tightness as mesh size tends to zero, identify subsequential limits, establish a domain Markov property and conformal covariance, and control boundary behavior. Crossing events must be transported measurably and their probabilities shown to converge. The local identity at $p=1/2$ selects the balanced parameter; scaling-limit theory describes the geometry that emerges when this balance persists across all scales.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The exact finite result is structurally informative. The cubic $3p^2-2p^3$ is a majority probability, a complement-symmetric map of the unit interval, and the one-face spanning probability for triangular bonds. Its unique fair point follows from symmetry plus a positive-factor argument.

The finite probability framework supplies reusable threshold tools: normalization is binomial, fixed-pattern probabilities are powers of $p$, expected counts are sums of those powers, union bounds turn small means into absence, and variance bounds turn robustly large means into presence.

Future work should construct finite planar patches and primal/dual alternatives; develop monotone couplings and product-measure correlation results; formalize matching-lattice transformations while keeping site and bond spaces distinct; establish pivotal-site sharp-threshold estimates; complete the triangular-site infinite-volume argument; treat square-bond duality separately; define and rigorously bound the square-site threshold without asserting an unsupported formula; and build scaling-limit and $\mathrm{SLE}_6$ machinery for conformal invariance.

\section{Scope and interpretive safeguards}
The word ``threshold'' is used at several levels. The equation $C(p)=1/2$ defines fairness for one finite event. A finite-size crossing threshold may instead be the value at which a rectangle-crossing probability reaches a chosen quantile. An infinite-volume critical probability is defined through the existence of an infinite cluster. Theorems may relate these quantities, but they are not synonymous by definition.

Likewise, polynomial equality does not imply model equivalence. The identity $B(p)=C(p)$ holds because both one-face events count successful subsets of size two or three among three Bernoulli objects. It gives no measure-preserving correspondence between site and bond configurations on an extended lattice. A global comparison must explicitly transport paths, boundaries, and probabilities.

Numerical evidence and exact proof also have different roles. Exact enumeration can certify a finite formula by including every configuration. Monte Carlo can estimate transitions in larger systems with quantified sampling error. Neither a plotted crossing nor a high-precision decimal supplies an analytic expression. For square-site percolation, the responsible conclusion is a numerical estimate together with rigorous bounds, not an unsupported closed form.

These safeguards isolate the missing mathematical steps and prevent a local symmetry from carrying global conclusions that require topology, correlation estimates, and limits.

\section{Conclusion}
For one triangular face,
\[
C(p)=3p^2-2p^3,
\qquad
C(1-p)=1-C(p),
\]
and, for $p\in[0,1]$,
\[
C(p)=\frac12\iff p=\frac12.
\]
The corresponding bond-spanning polynomial is identical. These statements completely settle the local self-duality calculation. Infinite-volume criticality still requires planar topology, sharpness, and limits; conformal invariance requires scaling-limit theory. The correct conclusion is both exact and restrained: triangular symmetry identifies a unique local fair parameter, while the square-site threshold remains without a known closed analytic form.

